# Cloudflare Pages/Workers asset upload — excludes files from the deployed
# static site, same syntax and purpose as .gitignore but read by Cloudflare's
# deploy step specifically. Needed because Cloudflare's build environment
# auto-runs `npm install` when it sees package.json (this repo only has one
# for the optional Capacitor Android tooling — the deployed site itself has
# no build step, see README), and if the "build output directory" is left as
# the repo root, the resulting node_modules/ gets swept up as a deployable
# asset. That's what causes the "asset too large" error on
# node_modules/workerd/bin/workerd (Cloudflare's own deploy tooling, not a
# dependency of this project) — excluding node_modules/ here is the fix.
#
# The Android project and its own node_modules-equivalents are also
# irrelevant to the deployed web app and excluded for the same reason.
node_modules/
www/
android/
package.json
package-lock.json
.git/

# Local dev state — Miniflare's cache/state directory and the file that
# actually holds real API keys for local `wrangler dev` testing (see
# README). Neither is meant to exist at deploy time, but excluding them
# here means a deploy run from a directory that still has one can't
# accidentally publish it as a static asset.
.wrangler/
.dev.vars

# worker.js is bundled directly via wrangler.jsonc's "main" field, and
# functions/ is server-side source (a Pages Functions entry point) — neither
# is meant to be fetchable as a plain static file.
#
# lib/ is NOT excluded, on purpose, even though most of its modules
# (resolve-maps-url.js, opencharge-poi.js, tomtom-*.js, valhalla-proxy.js,
# flights-proxy.js) are server-side-only, shared between worker.js and
# functions/api/*.js. Since app.js's own extracted-for-testability modules
# (format-utils.js, text-utils.js, google-maps-url.js, kochi-geo.js,
# stop-drag-utils.js, transit-labels.js — see tests/) live in this same
# directory and are `import`ed by app.js AT RUNTIME IN THE BROWSER,
# excluding lib/ here 404s those imports and breaks the entire app (an ES
# module import failure aborts the whole script) — confirmed live on the
# deployed Worker. None of the server-side proxy modules contain secrets
# (those are all read from Cloudflare env vars/secrets, never hardcoded),
# so there's no harm in the whole directory being publicly fetchable.
worker.js
wrangler.jsonc
functions/
